notes:
Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — async fetcher and cache are coherent designs that mirror the sync side faithfully. Two correctness bugs (cache time-source mismatch, rollback TOCTOU) are real; default-no-timeout reference clients are a real DoS footgun. None are unrecoverable pre-1.0; all are pre-launch-window fixes. The biggest risk is that the reference clients ship as the de-facto default before timeout defaults are added.
Confidence: 75% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: f1j4.2 (TOCTOU race — I have not constructed a stress test; the analysis is by reading; race may have a guard I missed in the InMemoryCache impl I didn't dig into deeply). Also f1j4.5 (replicated anchor-default footgun — sync pkix-revocation has it too and this could be a deliberate decision I'm second-guessing). Uncertainty driven by: (a) not having run the test suite to confirm behaviour matches docstrings, (b) not having inspected pkix-revocation's CrlChecker / OcspChecker internals to confirm the validity-window re-check that masks the f1j4.1 correctness bug actually exists in current code, (c) not having traced reqwest 0.12's exact timeout-default semantics — I'm asserting 'no default timeout' from prior knowledge of reqwest 0.11; 0.12 may have changed (low probability). Would raise confidence if: I could run `cargo test --all-features -p pkix-revocation-http` and confirm no behavioural regressions, AND read pkix-revocation/src/crl.rs to verify CrlChecker::new still re-checks the validity window after parse, AND skim reqwest 0.12.28's CHANGELOG for timeout-default changes.
